<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vasco da Gama</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1469–<a href="/years/1524">1524</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vasco da Gama was a Portuguese navigator who led the first European voyage to reach <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> by sea, arriving at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calicut">Calicut</a> in 1498 after rounding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a>. His voyage opened a direct maritime route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a> treat it as foundational, permanently redirecting the spice trade away from overland routes and Venetian intermediaries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama.jpg" alt="Vasco da Gama" width="309" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ignoto_portoghese,_ritratto_di_un_cavaliere_dell%27ordine_di_cristo,_1525-50_ca._02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Vasco da Gama was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-sine">Sines</a>, a seaport on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alentejo">Alentejo</a> coast of southwest Portugal. His father, Estêvão da Gama, had served as a knight of the household of Ferdinand, Duke of Viseu, rose in the ranks of the military <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-santiago">Order of Santiago</a> and was civil governor of Sines until 1478. Portuguese ships had worked down the African coast since the early 15th century, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolomeu-dias">Bartolomeu Dias</a> had found the South Atlantic westerlies in 1487.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On 8 July 1497 da Gama left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> with four ships and a crew of 170. After passing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a> he steered into the open ocean to catch the westerlies and made landfall on the African coast on 4 November 1497, having sailed more than 10,000 kilometres without touching land, the longest such journey made up to then. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a> in March 1498 he posed as a Muslim to gain an audience with the sultan, but a hostile crowd drove him out. At Malindi a Gujarati pilot guided the fleet across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>.</p><p>The fleet anchored near Kozhikode on 20 May 1498. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamorin">Zamorin</a> received da Gama, but the gifts were judged poor, Muslim merchants cast him as a pirate, and his request to leave a factor behind was turned down. The return crossing against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> took more than 90 days, against 23 on the way out. Only about 55 men came home, and two ships were lost.</p><p>In 1502 da Gama commanded the fourth Portuguese India armada. Off Madayi he captured the pilgrim ship Mirim, which carried over 400 people, including about 50 women, and burned it with them aboard, according to the eyewitness Thomé Lopes and the chronicler Gaspar Correia. He then bombarded Kozhikode. Back in Portugal in September 1503, he was passed over when the first viceroy of India was named in 1505.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After Manuel I died in late 1521, King John III appointed da Gama governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, with the title of viceroy, in 1524. Da Gama contracted malaria soon after arriving and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kochi">Cochin</a> on 24 December 1524. His body was returned to Portugal in 1539. His voyage opened a sea route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and India that the Portuguese held as a commercial monopoly for several decades. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luis-de-camoes">Luís de Camões</a> wrote the national epic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/os-lusiadas">Os Lusíadas</a> in his honour, while the killing of the pilgrims at sea has tempered his reputation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vasco da Gama” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Vasco da Gama</b> bombarded Mogadishu in 1499 on his return from India, and in 1507 a Portuguese fleet under Tristão da Cunha and Afonso de Albuquerque sacked Barawa, but the Portuguese never established lasting control over Mogadishu or the other Somali ports.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ajuran-sultanate">Ajuran Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1502 <b>Vasco da Gama</b>, commanding the fourth Portuguese fleet sent to India, forced Emir Ibrahim to agree to pay tribute under threat of attack.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kilwa-sultanate">Kilwa Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Vasco da Gama</b> reached Calicut in 1498, Pedro Álvares Cabral reached Brazil in 1500, and by mid-century Portuguese traders were in China and Japan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Vasco da Gama</b> came to Malindi in 1498 to sign a trade agreement and to hire a guide for the voyage to India; the padrão he erected, the Vasco da Gama Pillar, was in 1823 the only vestige of 'the once splendid city of Melinda' that a British…</blockquote><a href="/empire/malindi">Malindi · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>When <b>Vasco da Gama</b> arrived in April 1498, the town’s rulers were hostile and attempted to seize his ships, and he sailed on to Mombasa’s rival Malindi, which welcomed him.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mombasa">Mombasa · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Manuel I, <b>Vasco da Gama</b> completed that route in 1498, reaching Calicut on the Malabar Coast and returning to Lisbon with a cargo reputedly worth many times the expedition’s cost.</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Sines, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kochi">Kochi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.96,-8.86&amp;year=1504" aria-label="Sines on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4329 799 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Vasco da Gama"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4779 1069h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4779 1069h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.96,-8.86&amp;year=1504">What was here in 1504? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
